%!testlib
%!
% Flattening replaces a curve with straight lines, and PLRM 8.2 (setflat)
% says what that is allowed to cost: flatness is "the maximum allowable
% distance of any point of the approximation from the corresponding point
% on the true curve, measured in output device pixels". ANY point -- so
% whether the bound holds is a question about the whole of a piece, and a
% measure that looks at one place on the curve cannot answer it.
%
% One family makes the difference visible. Writing the curve against the
% chord that would replace it,
%
%     B(t) - L(t) = (1-t)^2 t u + (1-t) t^2 v,
%     u = 3 P1 - 2 P0 - P3,   v = 3 P2 - P0 - 2 P3,
%
% the gap at the middle is |u+v|/8 and vanishes identically when v = -u,
% that is for every curve whose two control points sit symmetrically about
% the middle of its chord (P1 + P2 = P0 + P3). Such curves are not close
% to their chords -- the first case below stands eight units from one --
% and the family is closed under every affine map, so no scale, rotation
% or setflat value leaves it. What bounds them is that both control points
% enter the measure, each against the chord: max(|u|,|v|)/4 is an upper
% bound on the gap at every t, and is 80 units where the middle gap is nil.
%
% Everything here is asked through flattenpath, pathbbox and pathforall,
% so no raster is involved and no second opinion is needed: the expected
% boxes are the curves' own extrema, solved from B'(t) = 0 and written in
% below to seven places.
%
% Cases D and E are controls and hold in both directions. Their boxes say
% that an ordinary curve is still flattened correctly, and the ceilings on
% their piece counts say it is still flattened about as cheaply -- a
% measure that were merely different, and coarser, would pass the boxes
% and fail the ceilings.

1 setflat

% -  segs  int
% The straight pieces one flattened curve became. A curve is one moveto
% and then a lineto per piece, so the linetos are the count.
/segs {
    /n 0 def
    { pop pop } { pop pop /n n 1 add def } { } { } pathforall
    n
} bind def

% x0 y0 x1 y1 (what)  bboxis  -
% The flattened path's box against the true curve's, allowed to differ by
% the flatness in force and no more. Both directions are real bounds: the
% vertices are points of the curve, so the box cannot exceed the true one,
% and no point of the approximation is further from the curve than the
% flatness, so it cannot fall short of it by more either.
/bboxis {
    /what exch def
    4 array astore /want exch def
    pathbbox 4 array astore /got exch def
    true
    0 1 3 {
        dup want exch get
        exch got exch get
        sub abs currentflat gt { pop false } if
    } for
    what assert
} bind def

% ---- the family a one-point measure cannot see

% P1 + P2 = P0 + P3, and the curve leaves the chord by eight units where
% one is allowed. y turns at t = (1 -/+ sqrt(3/5))/2.
newpath 0 0 moveto 100 -50 0 150 100 100 curveto flattenpath
segs 8 ge (a symmetric cubic is subdivided) assert
0 -8.0947502 100 108.0947502 (a symmetric cubic's flattened box) bboxis

% The same family with its extrema at the endpoints, so the box says
% nothing and the count carries the case on its own. A single line stands
% twenty-one units from this curve.
newpath 0 0 moveto 100 0 0 100 100 100 curveto flattenpath
segs 8 ge (a symmetric cubic whose box hides it is subdivided) assert

% P0 = P3: the chord is a point, and with it the whole flattened path.
% x turns at t = (1 -/+ sqrt(1/3))/2.
newpath 50 50 moveto 150 50 -50 50 50 50 curveto flattenpath
segs 8 ge (a closed loop is subdivided) assert
21.1324865 50 78.8675135 50 (a closed loop's flattened box) bboxis

% ---- controls: an ordinary curve is flattened as before

% The first case's P2 moved sideways, which breaks the symmetry and
% leaves y -- and so the box -- exactly as it was.
newpath 0 0 moveto 100 -50 40 150 100 100 curveto flattenpath
/dflt segs def
dflt 8 ge (an asymmetric cubic is subdivided) assert
dflt 40 le (an asymmetric cubic is not oversubdivided) assert
0 -8.0947502 100 108.0947502 (an asymmetric cubic's flattened box) bboxis

% A quarter circle of radius 100 about the origin, from -45 to +45
% degrees, with control points at 4/3 tan(45/2) of the radius along the
% tangents. It passes through (100, 0) at the middle, so its box reports
% the twenty-nine units of sagitta a chord would lose.
newpath 70.7106781 -70.7106781 moveto
        109.7631073 -31.6582489 109.7631073 31.6582489
        70.7106781 70.7106781 curveto flattenpath
segs 8 ge (a quarter arc is subdivided) assert
segs 24 le (a quarter arc is not oversubdivided) assert
70.7106781 -70.7106781 100 70.7106781 (a quarter arc's flattened box) bboxis

% ---- the flatness value is live, and does not buy the family back

100 setflat

% The first curve scaled by a hundred, alongside a flatness coarsened by
% a hundred. The measure is affine, so a family that collapsed at the
% default would collapse here too; this one strays eight hundred units
% where a hundred are allowed.
newpath 0 0 moveto 10000 -5000 0 15000 10000 10000 curveto flattenpath
segs 8 ge (a symmetric cubic is subdivided at a coarse flatness) assert
0 -809.47502 10000 10809.47502 (its flattened box at a coarse flatness) bboxis

% The control at the same coarse flatness, to show the parameter is read
% at all: a curve that needed many pieces at 1 needs few at 100.
newpath 0 0 moveto 100 -50 40 150 100 100 curveto flattenpath
segs dflt lt (a coarse flatness buys fewer pieces) assert

0.2 setflat
newpath 0 0 moveto 100 -50 40 150 100 100 curveto flattenpath
segs dflt gt (a fine flatness costs more pieces) assert

initgraphics
verdict
quit
